---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import SourcedBlock from "../../../components/SourcedBlock.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, computed, votes, chronology } from "../../../data/myanmar-genocide-case-2026-11-19-icj-rules-myanmar-committed-genocide.mjs";
const story = "myanmar-genocide-case--2026-11-19-icj-rules-myanmar-committed-genocide";
const fmt = computed.fmt;
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/myanmar-genocide-case/">Myanmar's genocide case</a><span>November 19, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Myanmar's genocide case &middot; November 19, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact" data-claims="ruling-genocide rohingya-group">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="ordered-remedies" /><Cite c="ordered-restitution" /><Cite c="no-enforcement" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#rohingya">What the court found was done to the Rohingya</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ruling">The ruling, finding by finding</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-got-here">How the case got here</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">The majority, the dissent and Myanmar</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">What the coverage got right and wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What Myanmar has to do now</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The International Court of Justice, the United Nations' court for disputes between countries, ruled on Nov. 19 that Myanmar committed genocide against the Rohingya, a long-persecuted Muslim minority, during the 2017 "clearance operations" by Myanmar's security forces in northern Rakhine State. The vote was {n.majority} to {n.minority}.<Cite c="ruling-genocide" /><Cite c="ruling-2017-operations" /><Cite c="rohingya-group" /> It is the first time the court has found that a country itself committed genocide.<Cite c="first-commit" /> The Gambia, a small, mostly Muslim country in West Africa backed by the {n.oicMembers}-member Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, brought the case in 2019 under the 1948 Genocide Convention, which lets any country that has signed it sue another for breaking it.<Cite c="gambia-who" /><Cite c="oic-backing" /><Cite c="gambia-filed" /><Cite c="any-state-may-sue" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The court found that Myanmar's security forces committed widespread rape, attacked children and shot at Rohingya as they fled, and that villages were destroyed and built over.<Cite c="findings-violence" /><Cite c="findings-villages" /> It accepted a UN fact-finding mission's estimate of at least {fmt(n.deadAtLeast)} dead, and more than {fmt(n.fled2017)} Rohingya fled to Bangladesh in 2017.<Cite c="deaths" /><Cite c="fled-2017" /> The majority concluded that the only reasonable explanation for what the forces did was an intent to destroy the roughly {fmt(n.targeted)} Rohingya who lived in northern Rakhine.<Cite c="majority-intent" /><Cite c="targeted-815000" /> Six judges dissented: they agreed soldiers killed and gravely harmed Rohingya in three villages, but said the evidence did not show that across northern Rakhine or prove intent to destroy the group.<Cite c="dissent-three-villages" /><Cite c="dissent-509" /><Cite c="dissent-intent" /> The man who commanded the military then, Senior General Min Aung Hlaing, led its 2021 coup and was declared Myanmar's president in April.<Cite c="mah-president" /><Cite c="coup-2021" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The judgment is final and cannot be appealed.<Cite c="ruling-final" /> It orders Myanmar to investigate and, where warranted, prosecute those responsible, to let the Rohingya return home and to compensate them, and leaves the amount for later, after Myanmar reports back within two years.<Cite c="ordered-remedies" /><Cite c="ordered-restitution" /><Cite c="report-two-years" /> The court cannot enforce any of it beyond reporting Myanmar to the UN Security Council.<Cite c="no-enforcement" /> Myanmar's government rejected it. "This decision is not a unanimous decision by everyone, it is not fair," its spokesperson, Khaing Khaing Soe, said on Nov. 20. "Our country was unjustly accused."<Cite c="myanmar-unfair" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="rohingya">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the court found was done to the Rohingya</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On Aug. 25, 2017, the Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army, an armed group, attacked at least {n.outposts} security posts across northern Rakhine, and the military's clearance operations began the same day.<Cite c="arsa-attacks" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Min Gyi was one of the villages the court examined. On the morning of Aug. 30, it found, soldiers killed civilians there, including children and people trying to flee, separated the men and executed them, committed widespread sexual violence, and burned people, bodies and homes, while the Rakhine part of the village was left largely untouched.<Cite c="min-gyi" /> Rohingya refugees counted {n.minGyiDead} dead there; the UN fact-finding mission found that count credible, and the court found it supported by the evidence.<Cite c="min-gyi-750" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Whether what happened at Min Gyi and two other villages shows one pattern across northern Rakhine is what the six dissenting judges dispute.<Cite c="dissent-509" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="ruling">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The ruling, finding by finding</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The genocide finding split the court {n.majority} to {n.minority}.<Cite c="ruling-genocide" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>What the {n.judges} judges decided</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="data-table-wrap">
            <table class="data-table vote-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Vote</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {votes.map((v) => (
                  <tr class={v.forMyanmar ? "vote-for-myanmar" : ""}>
                    <td>{claim(story, v.c).short}<Cite c={v.c} /></td>
                    <td>{v.vote}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="chart-source">From the court's judgment. The last line went in Myanmar's favor: the court found it did not incite private people to commit genocide, and counted any incitement of its own forces within the genocide finding itself.<Cite c="ruling-incite" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The convention lists acts that are genocide when done with intent to destroy a group. The court found three of them: killing, causing serious bodily or mental harm, and measures meant to prevent births. It did not find the fourth act The Gambia alleged, deliberately imposing living conditions meant to destroy the group.<Cite c="acts-found" /><Cite c="act-not-found" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">There was no direct evidence of intent, so the court said intent to destroy had to be the only reasonable inference from the facts.<Cite c="intent-test" /> Myanmar offered three other explanations: fighting insurgents, driving the Rohingya out, and punishing them. The majority rejected all three.<Cite c="majority-rejects-alternatives" /> On fighting insurgents, it found no evidence the forces told civilians from fighters; on driving the Rohingya out, no evidence of any government plan to move them; on punishment, that the scale and methods went well beyond what punishing them would take.<Cite c="majority-no-distinction" /><Cite c="majority-no-transfer-plan" /><Cite c="majority-beyond-punishment" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In January 2020 the court had issued an emergency order telling Myanmar to prevent genocidal acts and to preserve evidence.<Cite c="order-2020" /> It found Myanmar did not break the first two parts of that order but did break the third, to preserve evidence: the remains of at least five Rohingya villages were burned in May 2020.<Cite c="no-breach-first-measures" /><Cite c="breach-may-2020" /><Cite c="ruling-evidence-order" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="how-it-got-here">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">How the case got here</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the earlier expulsions to the ruling</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={chronology} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The majority, the dissent and Myanmar</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The nine in the majority</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The majority included the court's president, Iwasawa Yuji of Japan, who read out the judgment, and Navanethem Pillay, the judge The Gambia chose to sit on the case.<Cite c="president-iwasawa" /><Cite c="majority-judges" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="icj-myanmar-press-release-2026-11-19" detail>
          <p>The nine in the majority were President Iwasawa, Judges Charlesworth, Brant, Gómez Robledo, Cleveland, Aurescu, Tladi and Hmoud, and Navanethem Pillay, the judge The Gambia chose.<Cite c="majority-judges" /> The six against were Vice-President Sebutinde, Judges Tomka, Abraham, Xue and Nolte, and Claus Kress, the judge Myanmar chose.<Cite c="dissent-judges" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The six who dissented</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The six who voted against the genocide finding included the court's vice-president, Julia Sebutinde; Xue Hanqin, its Chinese judge; and Claus Kress, the judge Myanmar chose to sit on the case.<Cite c="sebutinde" /><Cite c="xue" /><Cite c="dissent-judges" /> They agreed with the majority that the Rohingya are a protected group and that the roughly {fmt(n.targeted)} in northern Rakhine were the part that counted.<Cite c="dissent-agree-group" /> They also accepted that soldiers killed Rohingya, including children, and caused them serious harm at Min Gyi and two other villages, the kinds of acts the convention lists, many of them going far beyond what fighting the insurgents required, but not proof of genocide on their own.<Cite c="dissent-three-villages" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Where they parted from the majority was on what that proved. Min Gyi and the other two were three of {n.villages} Rohingya villages in the region, they wrote, and the evidence did not show the same conduct repeated on a wider scale; attacks on fleeing Rohingya did sometimes occur, but were not shown to be systematic.<Cite c="dissent-509" /><Cite c="dissent-flight" /> Driving the Rohingya out, or punishing them collectively, could also explain the evidence, so in their view genocide was not established.<Cite c="dissent-intent" /> They also said the majority departed from the court's own test. In 2015, rejecting Croatia's genocide case against Serbia, the court asked whether intent to destroy was the only reasonable inference from a pattern of conduct, and found that the crimes against Croats appeared aimed at driving them out instead.<Cite c="croatia-test" /> The dissenters say the majority did not hold to that test here.<Cite c="dissent-test" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="The six dissenting judges" when="Nov. 19, 2026" where="Joint dissenting opinion">
          <p>"Our conclusion is that the commission of genocide by Myanmar has not been established."<Cite c="dissent-quote" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Myanmar</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Before the 2021 coup, Aung San Suu Kyi, then Myanmar's civilian leader, defended the military at the court.<Cite c="suu-kyi-defended" /> Myanmar's case has been that its forces were countering an insurgency by the Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army.<Cite c="myanmar-case" /> At the January hearings Myanmar's representative at the court, Ko Ko Hlaing, told the judges that Myanmar "was not obliged to remain idle and allow terrorists to have free rein."<Cite c="myanmar-agent-quote" /> A commission of enquiry Myanmar set up found war crimes and serious human rights violations in the 2017 operations, but no evidence that the killings or displacement were carried out with intent to destroy the Rohingya.<Cite c="icoe" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In rejecting the ruling, the government's spokesperson did not address what the court ordered Myanmar to do.<Cite c="myanmar-silent-on-orders" /> Myanmar's opposition National Unity Government said it respects the court's authority.<Cite c="nug-respects" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">In the camps</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In Bangladesh, Mohammed Yeasin, a Rohingya refugee whose wife and sister were killed in an August 2024 drone attack by the Rakhine armed group, said: "I am happy about the I.C.J. But I don't know when my sorrows will end."<Cite c="yeasin" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The Gambia</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Myanmar had argued that The Gambia was only suing for the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation; in 2020 the court ruled that outside support did not bar the case.<Cite c="proxy-rejected" /> After the ruling, The Gambia's attorney general, Dawda Jallow, said "we will stand with the Rohingya until justice is served."<Cite c="gambia-ag" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Myanmar was found responsible, not guilty<Cite c="first-commit" /><Cite c="not-criminal" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Al Jazeera's headline said the court "convicts Myanmar," and other outlets, including the UN's own news service, wrote "guilty."<Cite c="aj-convicts" /><Cite c="outlets-guilty" /><Cite c="un-news-guilty" /> The court says a country's responsibilities under the Genocide Convention are "not of a criminal nature."<Cite c="not-criminal" /> It is not a criminal court and cannot send military officials to prison.<Cite c="not-criminal-court" /> It ordered Myanmar to investigate and, where warranted, prosecute those responsible.<Cite c="ordered-remedies" /> Separately, the prosecutor of the International Criminal Court applied in 2024 for a warrant to arrest Min Aung Hlaing, alleging the crimes against humanity of deportation and persecution; no charges have been confirmed.<Cite c="icc-application" /> Amnesty International said on Nov. 19 that no decision on that request had been made public.<Cite c="icc-no-decision" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not the first country found to break the Genocide Convention<Cite c="serbia-2007-prevent" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The New York Times wrote that the ruling makes Myanmar "the first state to be held to account for violating the 1948 Genocide Convention."<Cite c="nyt-first" /> In 2007 the same court found that Serbia violated the convention by failing to prevent the genocide at Srebrenica, while finding that Serbia did not commit genocide itself.<Cite c="serbia-2007-prevent" /><Cite c="serbia-2007-not-committed" /> What is new on Nov. 19 is that the court found a country itself committed genocide.<Cite c="first-commit" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right and wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">AP described what is new correctly, as "the first time the court has ruled that a state committed genocide."<Cite c="ap-right" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Where reports of the ruling went wrong</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="data-table-wrap">
            <table class="data-table coverage-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Outlet</th><th>What it said</th><th>What the record shows</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>The New York Times</td><td>Myanmar is "the first state to be held to account for violating the 1948 Genocide Convention."<Cite c="nyt-first" /></td><td>Serbia, {n.serbiaYear}<Cite c="serbia-2007-prevent" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>The New York Times</td><td>"2016 and 2017, the years ruled a genocide."<Cite c="nyt-2016" /></td><td>Limited to the {n.findingYear} operations<Cite c="ruling-2017-operations" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>The Washington Post</td><td>"The first time the top court of the United Nations has ruled that a state violated the 1948 Genocide Convention."<Cite c="wapo-first" /></td><td>Serbia, {n.serbiaYear}<Cite c="serbia-2007-prevent" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>UPI</td><td>"Myanmar found guilty of genocide," and the first time the court "found a country guilty of violating" the convention.<Cite c="upi-words" /></td><td>Responsibility, not a crime; Serbia, {n.serbiaYear}<Cite c="not-criminal" /><Cite c="serbia-2007-prevent" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Al Jazeera</td><td>The court "convicts Myanmar."<Cite c="aj-convicts" /></td><td>Responsibility, not a crime<Cite c="not-criminal" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Al Jazeera</td><td>The court ruled that Myanmar "must cease all acts of violence."<Cite c="aj-must-cease" /></td><td>No such order<Cite c="no-cessation" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>DW and the Guardian's analysis</td><td>Myanmar found "guilty."<Cite c="dw-guardian-guilty" /></td><td>Responsibility, not a crime<Cite c="not-criminal" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>The Guardian</td><td>Min Aung Hlaing "has a pending arrest warrant" from the International Criminal Court.<Cite c="guardian-pending-warrant" /></td><td>Application only<Cite c="icc-application" /></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Human Rights Watch</td><td>Responsible for genocide during the 2016-2017 "clearance operations."<Cite c="hrw-2016" /></td><td>Limited to the {n.findingYear} operations<Cite c="ruling-2017-operations" /></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What Myanmar has to do now</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The court found Myanmar had failed to punish the crimes.<Cite c="ruling-punish" /> One case the court cites: seven soldiers and officers convicted over the killing of ten men at the village of Inn Din were sentenced to {n.sentenceYears} years, and Min Aung Hlaing pardoned them after eight months.<Cite c="inn-din-pardon" /> Myanmar must now investigate everyone reasonably suspected and, where warranted, prosecute them; pass laws that give the convention effect; and give guarantees it will not happen again.<Cite c="ordered-remedies" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">It must also make restitution, putting back what can be put back: let displaced Rohingya return home safely and voluntarily, return or replace their property, help find the missing and rebury the dead, and provide rehabilitation for survivors.<Cite c="ordered-restitution" /><Cite c="restitution-list" /> The court declined The Gambia's request to order citizenship for all Rohingya, but required Myanmar to protect them from discrimination, including in getting citizenship.<Cite c="no-citizenship" /><Cite c="citizenship-discrimination" /> It made no order to stop the genocide, because the genocide was not found to be ongoing.<Cite c="no-cessation" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Return depends on more than Myanmar's government: much of the Rohingya homeland is now held by the Arakan Army, an ethnic Rakhine armed group, which has also been accused of persecuting Rohingya.<Cite c="arakan-army" /> About {n.refugeesNowMillions} million Rohingya refugees now live in Bangladesh.<Cite c="refugees-now" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Within two years Myanmar must report to the court on the restitution it has made and, where restitution cannot repair the harm, the compensation it proposes. The court will then hear both sides and decide how the amount is set.<Cite c="report-two-years" /> If Myanmar does not comply, The Gambia can go to the Security Council, where any of the permanent members, which include China and Russia, can block action.<Cite c="charter-94" /><Cite c="charter-veto" /> China's foreign ministry said it was concerned about "jurisdiction overreach and expanded interpretation."<Cite c="china-reaction" /> Britain's foreign secretary, Ed Miliband, said Britain was expelling Myanmar's defence attaché and called for an urgent Security Council session.<Cite c="uk-reaction" /> The US State Department said it was examining the judgment and warned against "politicisation" of the court.<Cite c="us-reaction" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .coverage-table td:first-child { white-space: nowrap; font-weight: 600; }
  .vote-table td:last-child { white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .vote-for-myanmar td { color: var(--slate); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .coverage-table td:first-child { white-space: normal; }
  }
</style>
